1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,315
Two hundred and forty Excel files.

2
00:00:02,315 --> 00:00:05,291
One for every store, for every month of the year.

3
00:00:05,291 --> 00:00:05,655
Watch.

4
00:00:05,655 --> 00:00:11,486
In about two seconds, Python opens every single one, and stacks them into one clean spreadsheet.

5
00:00:11,486 --> 00:00:15,981
Five thousand seven hundred and eighteen rows, and not one copy and paste.

6
00:00:16,681 --> 00:00:21,521
Today you'll build that script, step by step, even if you've never written Python before.

7
00:00:21,521 --> 00:00:25,926
You'll read one Excel file, then a whole folder, and save the result as one file.

8
00:00:25,926 --> 00:00:34,247
And I'll show you the three mistakes that break this script for almost everyone, including one that gives you the wrong numbers without any error at all.

9
00:00:34,947 --> 00:00:35,899
Here's the plan.

10
00:00:35,899 --> 00:00:37,506
Install two free libraries.

11
00:00:37,506 --> 00:00:40,124
Read one file, to see what pandas gives you.

12
00:00:40,124 --> 00:00:41,910
Find every file in the folder.

13
00:00:41,910 --> 00:00:43,695
Stack them together, and save.

14
00:00:43,695 --> 00:00:48,694
Then check the result, because a merge that runs is not always a merge that's right.

15
00:00:49,394 --> 00:00:52,297
Why not just open each file and copy the rows over?

16
00:00:52,297 --> 00:00:53,549
With five files, sure.

17
00:00:53,549 --> 00:01:00,095
With two hundred and forty, that's an afternoon of clicking, and it's very easy to skip a file, or paste one twice.

18
00:01:00,095 --> 00:01:02,713
And next month, you'd have to do it all again.

19
00:01:02,713 --> 00:01:06,299
A script does the same job the same way every time, in seconds.

20
00:01:06,999 --> 00:01:10,213
You need Python installed, free from python dot org.

21
00:01:10,213 --> 00:01:13,489
Open a terminal and type pip install pandas openpyxl.

22
00:01:13,489 --> 00:01:16,270
Pandas is the library that works with tables.

23
00:01:16,270 --> 00:01:19,916
Openpyxl is what pandas uses to read and write Excel files.

24
00:01:19,916 --> 00:01:22,018
Without it, read excel won't work.

25
00:01:22,018 --> 00:01:24,181
Then check both with this one line.

26
00:01:24,181 --> 00:01:26,591
Two version numbers means you're ready.

27
00:01:27,292 --> 00:01:28,753
Here's our project folder.

28
00:01:28,753 --> 00:01:33,475
Inside reports, there are two hundred and forty workbooks, named by month and store.

29
00:01:33,475 --> 00:01:38,310
There's also one workbook with several sheets, which we'll use for a bonus at the end.

30
00:01:38,310 --> 00:01:41,402
Your scripts go right here, next to the reports folder.

31
00:01:42,102 --> 00:01:44,663
List the folder, and you can see the pattern.

32
00:01:44,663 --> 00:01:47,965
Twenty twenty six, dash, the month, then the store number.

33
00:01:47,965 --> 00:01:50,072
Two hundred and forty files in total.

34
00:01:50,072 --> 00:01:52,576
Every one of them has the same four columns.

35
00:01:52,576 --> 00:01:54,398
Date, product, units, and price.

36
00:01:55,098 --> 00:01:55,631
Step one.

37
00:01:55,631 --> 00:01:59,127
Before touching two hundred and forty files, read just one.

38
00:01:59,127 --> 00:02:00,253
Create peek dot py.

39
00:02:00,253 --> 00:02:03,749
Import pandas as p d, that's the short name everybody uses.

40
00:02:03,749 --> 00:02:08,192
Then p d dot read excel, with the path to one file, gives you a data frame.

41
00:02:08,192 --> 00:02:10,088
That's pandas' word for a table.

42
00:02:10,088 --> 00:02:12,814
Print df dot head, to see the first five rows.

43
00:02:12,814 --> 00:02:14,947
And df dot shape tells you the size.

44
00:02:15,647 --> 00:02:16,069
Run it.

45
00:02:16,069 --> 00:02:19,507
There's the first store's January sales, as a neat table.

46
00:02:19,507 --> 00:02:23,246
The numbers on the left are just row numbers that pandas adds.

47
00:02:23,246 --> 00:02:27,649
And the shape, nineteen comma four, means nineteen rows and four columns.

48
00:02:27,649 --> 00:02:30,363
If this works, the hard part is already done.

49
00:02:30,363 --> 00:02:32,594
Merging is just doing this in a loop.

50
00:02:33,294 --> 00:02:33,834
Step two.

51
00:02:33,834 --> 00:02:35,092
Create merge dot p y.

52
00:02:35,092 --> 00:02:36,650
From pathlib, import Path.

53
00:02:36,650 --> 00:02:43,063
Path, reports, dot glob, star dot x l s x, finds every file in the reports folder that ends in dot x l s x.

54
00:02:43,063 --> 00:02:44,502
The star means any name.

55
00:02:44,502 --> 00:02:49,776
Sorted puts them in alphabetical order, so the result is the same every time you run it.

56
00:02:49,776 --> 00:02:51,873
Then print how many files it found.

57
00:02:51,873 --> 00:02:55,170
That one number will save you a lot of confusion later.

58
00:02:55,870 --> 00:02:56,964
Step three, the loop.

59
00:02:56,964 --> 00:02:59,048
Start with an empty list, called frames.

60
00:02:59,048 --> 00:03:02,175
For each file, read it with read excel, exactly like before.

61
00:03:02,175 --> 00:03:05,250
Then add a new column, called Source, with the file's name.

62
00:03:05,250 --> 00:03:07,803
That way, every row remembers where it came from.

63
00:03:07,803 --> 00:03:12,441
Trust me, when a number looks wrong later, this column is how you find the file to blame.

64
00:03:12,441 --> 00:03:14,161
And append the table to the list.

65
00:03:14,861 --> 00:03:15,466
Step four.

66
00:03:15,466 --> 00:03:20,127
P d dot concat takes the list of tables, and stacks them one under the other.

67
00:03:20,127 --> 00:03:26,423
Ignore index equals true renumbers the rows from zero, so you don't get two hundred and forty row zeros.

68
00:03:26,423 --> 00:03:27,936
Print the number of rows.

69
00:03:27,936 --> 00:03:32,053
Then to excel saves everything into one file, all sales dot x l s x.

70
00:03:32,053 --> 00:03:34,838
Index equals false leaves out the row numbers.

71
00:03:35,538 --> 00:03:38,358
Save, and run python three merge dot p y.

72
00:03:38,358 --> 00:03:40,766
Files found, two hundred and forty.

73
00:03:40,766 --> 00:03:44,000
Rows, five thousand seven hundred and eighteen.

74
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:45,926
Saved all sales dot x l s x.

75
00:03:45,926 --> 00:03:46,614
That's it.

76
00:03:46,614 --> 00:03:48,197
That's the whole merge.

77
00:03:48,897 --> 00:03:51,270
Here's what just happened, file by file.

78
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:56,374
Each workbook is read, gets its Source name, and lands at the bottom of the big table.

79
00:03:56,374 --> 00:03:57,382
But look closely.

80
00:03:57,382 --> 00:03:59,044
One file just turned yellow.

81
00:03:59,044 --> 00:04:00,646
Something in it didn't fit.

82
00:04:00,646 --> 00:04:02,367
And Python didn't say a word.

83
00:04:03,067 --> 00:04:04,898
This is why step five matters.

84
00:04:04,898 --> 00:04:06,180
Always check a merge.

85
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:10,574
Create check dot p y, read the merged file back, and print three things.

86
00:04:10,574 --> 00:04:16,250
The shape, the column names, and is n a dot sum, which counts the empty cells in each column.

87
00:04:16,250 --> 00:04:18,692
N a means not available, an empty value.

88
00:04:19,391 --> 00:04:20,882
And there's the problem.

89
00:04:20,882 --> 00:04:23,614
We expected five columns, but there are six.

90
00:04:23,614 --> 00:04:26,346
A column called Qty appeared out of nowhere.

91
00:04:26,346 --> 00:04:30,693
Units has twenty five empty cells, and Qty is empty almost everywhere.

92
00:04:30,693 --> 00:04:35,101
So when you add up units, you'd be missing sales, and you'd never know.

93
00:04:35,802 --> 00:04:36,891
Which file did it?

94
00:04:36,891 --> 00:04:39,372
Thanks to the Source column, that's easy.

95
00:04:39,372 --> 00:04:43,064
Keep the rows where units is empty, and count them by source.

96
00:04:43,064 --> 00:04:43,608
One file.

97
00:04:43,608 --> 00:04:45,000
Store seven, September.

98
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,029
Twenty five rows.

99
00:04:46,729 --> 00:04:49,307
Open that file, and the mystery is solved.

100
00:04:49,307 --> 00:04:52,438
Somebody typed Qty in the header, instead of Units.

101
00:04:52,438 --> 00:04:58,761
To pandas, those are two different columns, so concat keeps both, and fills the gaps with empty values.

102
00:04:58,761 --> 00:04:59,989
The fix is one line.

103
00:04:59,989 --> 00:05:03,119
Right after reading each file, rename Qty to Units.

104
00:05:03,119 --> 00:05:05,575
Now every file speaks the same language.

105
00:05:06,275 --> 00:05:07,280
Mistake number two.

106
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:09,450
You run the script from the wrong folder.

107
00:05:09,450 --> 00:05:12,042
Here I moved up one level, and ran it from there.

108
00:05:12,042 --> 00:05:12,995
Files found, zero.

109
00:05:12,995 --> 00:05:14,212
And then a scary error.

110
00:05:14,212 --> 00:05:16,275
Value error, no objects to concatenate.

111
00:05:16,275 --> 00:05:18,021
It just means the list was empty.

112
00:05:18,021 --> 00:05:21,619
Python looked for a reports folder where you are, and found nothing.

113
00:05:21,619 --> 00:05:23,841
Use c d to go to the project folder first.

114
00:05:23,841 --> 00:05:26,593
And that's exactly why we print the number of files.

115
00:05:27,293 --> 00:05:30,378
Mistake number three, and this one gets everybody.

116
00:05:30,378 --> 00:05:34,141
Leave one of the workbooks open in Excel, and run the script.

117
00:05:34,141 --> 00:05:38,768
Excel quietly creates a hidden file next to it, starting with tilde dollar.

118
00:05:38,768 --> 00:05:41,236
It ends in x l s x, so glob picks it up.

119
00:05:41,236 --> 00:05:43,025
But it isn't a real workbook.

120
00:05:43,025 --> 00:05:44,197
Watch what happens.

121
00:05:44,897 --> 00:05:49,282
Two hundred and forty files read, and then it crashes on the very last one.

122
00:05:49,282 --> 00:05:52,906
Files found, two hundred and forty one, one more than we have.

123
00:05:52,906 --> 00:05:56,355
And the error says, Excel file format cannot be determined.

124
00:05:56,355 --> 00:05:59,395
Close the workbook in Excel and the file disappears.

125
00:05:59,395 --> 00:06:01,909
But it's better to make the script skip it.

126
00:06:02,609 --> 00:06:04,998
Here's the final script, with both fixes.

127
00:06:04,998 --> 00:06:11,932
Inside the loop, if the file name starts with tilde dollar, print a message and continue, which jumps to the next file.

128
00:06:11,932 --> 00:06:14,146
Then the rename, so Qty becomes Units.

129
00:06:14,146 --> 00:06:17,992
And one extra print at the end, the total count of missing values.

130
00:06:17,992 --> 00:06:20,964
If that number isn't zero, you know to look closer.

131
00:06:21,664 --> 00:06:23,991
Run it, with the lock file still there.

132
00:06:23,991 --> 00:06:26,317
Files found, two hundred and forty one.

133
00:06:26,317 --> 00:06:27,451
Skipping lock file.

134
00:06:27,451 --> 00:06:30,255
Rows, five thousand seven hundred and eighteen.

135
00:06:30,255 --> 00:06:31,508
Missing values, zero.

136
00:06:32,208 --> 00:06:33,739
Same run, file by file.

137
00:06:33,739 --> 00:06:42,798
The lock file is skipped and crossed out, store seven's Qty column is renamed on the way in, and every row lands under the right header.

138
00:06:43,498 --> 00:06:44,885
And the check agrees.

139
00:06:44,885 --> 00:06:48,055
Five columns, all of them with zero empty cells.

140
00:06:48,055 --> 00:06:49,970
Now you can trust the totals.

141
00:06:50,670 --> 00:06:51,127
Pro tip.

142
00:06:51,127 --> 00:06:54,780
Once everything is in one table, the report is a few lines away.

143
00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:57,006
Multiply units by price to get revenue.

144
00:06:57,006 --> 00:07:02,828
The store number is inside the file name, characters eight to fifteen, so string slicing pulls it out.

145
00:07:02,828 --> 00:07:04,883
Group by store, and sum the revenue.

146
00:07:04,883 --> 00:07:08,079
Then an Excel writer saves two sheets into one workbook.

147
00:07:08,079 --> 00:07:10,306
All the rows, and the summary by store.

148
00:07:11,006 --> 00:07:11,499
Run it.

149
00:07:11,499 --> 00:07:15,939
The top five stores by revenue, with store nine in first place.

150
00:07:15,939 --> 00:07:20,380
And report dot x l s x, with both sheets inside, ready to send.

151
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:21,440
Bonus.

152
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:25,404
What if the data is in one workbook, but split across many sheets?

153
00:07:25,404 --> 00:07:29,307
Read excel with sheet name equals None reads every sheet at once.

154
00:07:29,307 --> 00:07:32,190
You get a dictionary, sheet name, and its table.

155
00:07:32,190 --> 00:07:36,214
Concat on the dictionary's values stacks them, exactly like before.

156
00:07:36,914 --> 00:07:41,637
Four sheets, quarter one to quarter four, and one table with twenty four rows.

157
00:07:41,637 --> 00:07:44,302
Want to know which sheet each row came from?

158
00:07:44,302 --> 00:07:47,753
Pass the whole dictionary to concat, and name the levels.

159
00:07:47,753 --> 00:07:51,024
Reset index turns the sheet name into a normal column.

160
00:07:51,724 --> 00:07:53,317
And how fast is all this?

161
00:07:53,317 --> 00:07:57,267
I timed the final script on these two hundred and forty files.

162
00:07:57,267 --> 00:08:00,645
Two point three seconds, including saving the result.

163
00:08:00,645 --> 00:08:04,404
By hand, at even thirty seconds per file, that's two hours.

164
00:08:05,104 --> 00:08:06,458
Here's your cheat sheet.

165
00:08:06,458 --> 00:08:07,811
Read excel for one file.

166
00:08:07,811 --> 00:08:09,052
Glob to find them all.

167
00:08:09,052 --> 00:08:11,816
A Source column to remember where rows came from.

168
00:08:11,816 --> 00:08:12,718
Concat to stack.

169
00:08:12,718 --> 00:08:14,523
Rename when headers don't match.

170
00:08:14,523 --> 00:08:17,230
And skip anything that starts with tilde dollar.

171
00:08:17,930 --> 00:08:18,722
Quick recap.

172
00:08:18,722 --> 00:08:20,571
Install pandas and openpyxl.

173
00:08:20,571 --> 00:08:21,891
Read one file first.

174
00:08:21,891 --> 00:08:26,446
Glob finds every file, and a loop reads them, adding a Source column.

175
00:08:26,446 --> 00:08:29,615
Concat stacks them, and to excel saves one file.

176
00:08:29,615 --> 00:08:32,190
Then always check, with is n a dot sum.

177
00:08:32,890 --> 00:08:36,802
Next on Shortcut Lab, we take this merged table, and clean it.

178
00:08:36,802 --> 00:08:43,807
Extra spaces, duplicates, dates written five different ways, and empty cells, fixed with pandas in a few lines.

179
00:08:43,807 --> 00:08:45,826
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
